# Line endings: LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every OS.
#
# Every file here is already committed as LF. What was missing is a statement
# of intent, which left the working-tree half of the question up to whatever
# `core.autocrlf` each clone happened to have. On a Windows clone with
# `core.autocrlf=true` Git wants a CRLF working tree, so it reported all ~60
# text files as modified — each with an empty diff, because it was flagging a
# conversion it intended to perform rather than a change anyone had made. Any
# command that rewrites mtimes (a Prettier pass will do it) surfaces the whole
# set at once, which is alarming and buries real changes.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone: Prettier writes LF by default and
# `prettier --check` is the lint gate in CI, so a CRLF working tree would fight
# the formatter. This pins both halves and overrides local `core.autocrlf`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets — never transform. Git's auto-detection would very likely get
# these right on its own, but a mangled guitar sample is a silent corruption
# and the explicit line costs nothing.
*.mp3 binary
*.png binary
*.webp binary
